1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,664
Holly:

2
00:00:00,664 --> 00:00:04,750
Hello Dr Green – I’m here to talk to you about my work placement.

3
00:00:04,750 --> 00:00:05,340
Tutor:

4
00:00:05,340 --> 00:00:07,110
Oh yes, it’s Holly, isn’t it?

5
00:00:07,110 --> 00:00:07,855
Holly:

6
00:00:07,855 --> 00:00:08,020
Yes.

7
00:00:08,020 --> 00:00:08,526
Tutor:

8
00:00:08,526 --> 00:00:08,607
So,

9
00:00:08,607 --> 00:00:10,650
which work placement have you chosen?

10
00:00:10,650 --> 00:00:11,392
Holly:

11
00:00:11,392 --> 00:00:14,902
I decided to go for the Orion Stadium placement.

12
00:00:14,902 --> 00:00:19,628
The event I’ll be managing is one where I’m helping to set up a

13
00:00:19,628 --> 00:00:21,700
sports competition for primary school children.

14
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:21,750
Tutor:

15
00:00:21,750 --> 00:00:22,315
Yup.

16
00:00:22,315 --> 00:00:26,620
That’s always a popular placement – even though it can be tougher than you

17
00:00:26,620 --> 00:00:27,850
think working with children.

18
00:00:27,850 --> 00:00:28,191
Holly:

19
00:00:28,191 --> 00:00:28,872
I know,

20
00:00:28,872 --> 00:00:33,983
but it’s the fresh air that attracts me – organising something indoors doesn’t have

21
00:00:33,983 --> 00:00:37,050
the same appeal, even though it might be fun.

22
00:00:37,050 --> 00:00:37,380
Tutor:

23
00:00:37,380 --> 00:00:37,710
OK,

24
00:00:37,710 --> 00:00:42,330
so obviously safety’s going to be one of your key concerns for this

25
00:00:42,330 --> 00:00:42,660
event.

26
00:00:42,660 --> 00:00:43,003
Holly:

27
00:00:43,003 --> 00:00:45,408
Yes, I’ve already thought about that.

28
00:00:45,408 --> 00:00:49,530
I’ll need to make sure none of the equipment’s damaged.

29
00:00:49,530 --> 00:00:49,893
Tutor:

30
00:00:49,893 --> 00:00:52,797
Ah well, you’ll be working with schools,

31
00:00:52,797 --> 00:00:55,337
so the equipment will be their responsibility.

32
00:00:55,337 --> 00:00:59,330
However, the grounds and what goes on there will be yours.

33
00:00:59,330 --> 00:00:59,670
Holly:

34
00:00:59,670 --> 00:01:05,450
Oh I see – that’ll include keeping everyone within the boundary once they’re in their kit

35
00:01:05,450 --> 00:01:06,810
and on the field?

36
00:01:06,810 --> 00:01:07,170
Tutor:

37
00:01:07,170 --> 00:01:12,205
Exactly – you’ll need to inspect areas like changing rooms as well for anything

38
00:01:12,205 --> 00:01:13,643
someone can trip over,

39
00:01:13,643 --> 00:01:17,240
but your main priority will be not to lose anyone!

40
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:17,646
Holly:

41
00:01:17,646 --> 00:01:18,052
Right.

42
00:01:18,052 --> 00:01:21,300
I’ll need staff to help with that.

43
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:21,620
Tutor:

44
00:01:21,620 --> 00:01:23,860
And don’t forget about the spectators.

45
00:01:23,860 --> 00:01:24,162
Holly:

46
00:01:24,162 --> 00:01:24,464
Mmm.

47
00:01:24,464 --> 00:01:27,487
I was thinking that many of them will be parents,

48
00:01:27,487 --> 00:01:29,300
who could help run the event.

49
00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:29,609
Tutor:

50
00:01:29,609 --> 00:01:31,462
I wouldn’t rely on that.

51
00:01:31,462 --> 00:01:34,860
They’ll be more interested in filming their children than volunteering.

52
00:01:34,860 --> 00:01:35,118
Holly:

53
00:01:35,118 --> 00:01:38,730
I’ll need to make sure they don’t interfere with events doing that!

54
00:01:38,730 --> 00:01:39,043
Tutor:

55
00:01:39,043 --> 00:01:45,300
And that’s not always easy, especially when a proud parent’s trying to get a snap of their child

56
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:47,490
and you want them to move elsewhere.

57
00:01:47,490 --> 00:01:48,140
Holly:

58
00:01:48,140 --> 00:01:48,790
OK.

59
00:01:48,790 --> 00:01:51,390
What about the scheduling?

60
00:01:51,390 --> 00:01:51,744
Tutor:

61
00:01:51,744 --> 00:01:56,707
With sporting events there are all sorts of things that can alter the timetable

62
00:01:56,707 --> 00:02:01,670
– like rain, for instance – though so far, we’ve always been lucky with that.

63
00:02:01,670 --> 00:02:02,015
Holly:

64
00:02:02,015 --> 00:02:02,359
Yeah,

65
00:02:02,359 --> 00:02:07,184
and I was thinking about what to do if someone got hurt as well.

66
00:02:07,184 --> 00:02:10,630
I know that last year that caused a terrible delay.

67
00:02:10,630 --> 00:02:10,933
Tutor:

68
00:02:10,933 --> 00:02:13,360
You have to be prepared for such things.

69
00:02:13,360 --> 00:02:13,718
Holly:

70
00:02:13,718 --> 00:02:14,075
Oh.

71
00:02:14,075 --> 00:02:18,722
What if a match ends in a draw – do you let the teams

72
00:02:18,722 --> 00:02:20,510
keep going until someone wins?

73
00:02:20,510 --> 00:02:20,783
Tutor:

74
00:02:20,783 --> 00:02:24,610
That’ll be up to you – and again, you need to plan for it.

75
00:02:24,610 --> 00:02:25,805
Holly:

76
00:02:25,805 --> 00:02:27,000
Right.

77
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:27,336
Tutor:

78
00:02:27,336 --> 00:02:32,047
Now, the aim of your work placement is to give you the opportunity to

79
00:02:32,047 --> 00:02:34,738
develop the skills that an events manager needs.

80
00:02:34,738 --> 00:02:37,430
So, let’s talk about those a bit.

81
00:02:37,430 --> 00:02:37,778
Holly:

82
00:02:37,778 --> 00:02:40,915
Well, I think my communication skills are pretty good.

83
00:02:40,915 --> 00:02:44,748
I can talk on the phone to people and book venues

84
00:02:44,748 --> 00:02:46,490
and that kind of thing.

85
00:02:46,490 --> 00:02:46,829
Tutor:

86
00:02:46,829 --> 00:02:50,553
Good – just remember it isn’t only about what you say.

87
00:02:50,553 --> 00:02:55,293
If you meet someone face-to-face and want to persuade them to be a sponsor,

88
00:02:55,293 --> 00:02:55,970
for example …

89
00:02:55,970 --> 00:02:56,263
Holly:

90
00:02:56,263 --> 00:02:58,317
Oh, I’ll dress up for that!

91
00:02:58,317 --> 00:02:58,610
Sure.

92
00:02:58,610 --> 00:02:58,980
Tutor:

93
00:02:58,980 --> 00:02:59,350
Good.

94
00:02:59,350 --> 00:03:03,050
Let’s go on to think about your organisational skills.

95
00:03:03,050 --> 00:03:08,970
You’re working in a very people-based industry and that means things won’t always go

96
00:03:08,970 --> 00:03:09,710
to plan.

97
00:03:09,710 --> 00:03:10,029
Holly:

98
00:03:10,029 --> 00:03:13,540
I guess it’s being prepared to make changes that matters.

99
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:13,843
Tutor:

100
00:03:13,843 --> 00:03:14,752
That’s right.

101
00:03:14,752 --> 00:03:18,086
You may have to make an on-the-spot change to a timetable

102
00:03:18,086 --> 00:03:20,510
because of a problem you hadn’t anticipated …

103
00:03:20,510 --> 00:03:21,008
Holly:

104
00:03:21,008 --> 00:03:22,502
… just do it!

105
00:03:22,502 --> 00:03:23,000
OK.

106
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:23,336
Tutor:

107
00:03:23,336 --> 00:03:25,690
How’s your time management these days?

108
00:03:25,690 --> 00:03:25,984
Holly:

109
00:03:25,984 --> 00:03:30,101
I’m working on it – I’m certainly better when I have a deadline,

110
00:03:30,101 --> 00:03:32,160
which is why this work suits me.

111
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:32,492
Tutor:

112
00:03:32,492 --> 00:03:32,823
Yes,

113
00:03:32,823 --> 00:03:36,140
but it’s how you respond as that deadline approaches!

114
00:03:36,140 --> 00:03:36,389
Holly:

115
00:03:36,389 --> 00:03:39,881
I know I’ve got to look calm even if I’m in a

116
00:03:39,881 --> 00:03:40,130
panic.

117
00:03:40,130 --> 00:03:40,433
Tutor:

118
00:03:40,433 --> 00:03:44,070
Just think to yourself – no one must know I’m under pressure.

119
00:03:44,070 --> 00:03:44,510
Holly:

120
00:03:44,510 --> 00:03:48,030
Yeah – even though I’m multi-tasking like crazy!

121
00:03:48,030 --> 00:03:48,364
Tutor:

122
00:03:48,364 --> 00:03:51,040
Another skill that events managers need is creativity.

123
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:54,718
Often your client has what we call the ‘big picture’ idea,

124
00:03:54,718 --> 00:03:59,734
but it’s up to the events manager to think of all the fine points

125
00:03:59,734 --> 00:04:01,740
that go to making it work.

126
00:04:01,740 --> 00:04:02,088
Holly:

127
00:04:02,088 --> 00:04:02,436
Right,

128
00:04:02,436 --> 00:04:07,304
so I need to listen carefully to that idea and then fill in all

129
00:04:07,304 --> 00:04:08,000
the gaps.

130
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:08,383
Tutor:

131
00:04:08,383 --> 00:04:09,532
That’s right.

132
00:04:09,532 --> 00:04:12,980
And you’ll have a team working under you,

133
00:04:12,980 --> 00:04:15,279
so another key skill is leadership.

134
00:04:15,279 --> 00:04:18,344
Your team may have lots of ideas too,

135
00:04:18,344 --> 00:04:21,792
but you’ve got to make the ultimate choices.

136
00:04:21,792 --> 00:04:25,240
Do we have refreshments inside or out, for example?

137
00:04:25,240 --> 00:04:25,594
Holly:

138
00:04:25,594 --> 00:04:28,070
Isn’t it better to be democratic?

139
00:04:28,070 --> 00:04:28,432
Tutor:

140
00:04:28,432 --> 00:04:30,244
It’s a nice idea,

141
00:04:30,244 --> 00:04:32,417
but you have the ultimate responsibility.

142
00:04:32,417 --> 00:04:34,953
So, believe in what you think best.

143
00:04:34,953 --> 00:04:36,765
Be prepared to say ‘yes’,

144
00:04:36,765 --> 00:04:40,750
that’s a good idea but it won’t work here.

145
00:04:40,750 --> 00:04:41,090
Holly:

146
00:04:41,090 --> 00:04:42,792
I see what you mean.

147
00:04:42,792 --> 00:04:45,174
What about the networking side of things?

148
00:04:45,174 --> 00:04:49,597
I know it’s an area that a lot of students worry about

149
00:04:49,597 --> 00:04:53,000
because we don’t have much experience to offer others.

150
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:53,400
Tutor:

151
00:04:53,400 --> 00:04:59,000
But even without it – you can still be an interesting person with useful ideas.

152
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:02,200
And the more people you impress, the better.

153
00:05:02,200 --> 00:05:02,444
Holly:

154
00:05:02,444 --> 00:05:05,620
I guess that will help me when I apply for a real job.

155
00:05:05,620 --> 00:05:06,042
Tutor:

156
00:05:06,042 --> 00:05:11,530
Exactly – think ahead – remember what your ambitions are and keep them in mind.

157
00:05:11,530 --> 00:05:12,265
Holly:

158
00:05:12,265 --> 00:05:13,000
Definitely.
